<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Sawangkhaburi regime</h1><p class="st-meta">1767 CE – 1770 CE · capital Sawangkhaburi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A northern Siamese regime led by a militant monk, Chao Phra Fang, which ruled from Sawangkhaburi after Ayutthaya fell.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom</a> in 1767, a senior monk known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chao-phra-fang">Chao Phra Fang</a>, formerly abbot in Fang (Sawangkhaburi, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttaradit">Uttaradit</a>), gathered followers and established his own regime in the upper Nan river region, retaining his monk's robes while exercising political power.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The regime was unusual in being led by monks: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chao-phra-fang">Chao Phra Fang</a> and senior monks among his followers held both religious and military authority. In 1768 it absorbed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/phitsanulok-regime">Phitsanulok regime</a> after the death of its ruler. Its power rested on control of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> lands and the loyalty of local troops.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1770 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">Taksin</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thonburi-kingdom">Thonburi Kingdom</a> marched north and captured Sawangkhaburi. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chao-phra-fang">Chao Phra Fang</a> fled and disappeared. Taksin then purged and reformed the northern sangha, re-ordaining monks whose conduct was doubted.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="theocracy, 1767 CE – 1770 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 82 V46 Q50 12 70 46 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 30 V82 M40 58 H60"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">theocracy</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mainland Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:94.87%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1767 CE – 1770 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sawangkhaburi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Chao Phra Fang</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Thai</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chao Phra Fang regime, Fang regime</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1768&amp;focus=sawangkhaburi-regime" aria-label="Sawangkhaburi regime on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7445 1610 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sawangkhaburi regime lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7728 1809h0M7752 1900h0M7753 1923h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7765 1866h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7765 1866h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Thailand. <a href="/atlas?year=1768&amp;focus=sawangkhaburi-regime">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
